  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 353 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThe cold front has pushed through all of Missouri during the \novernight while the center of the surface cyclone heads toward the \nGreat Lakes Region. Center of H5 closed-low is currently positioned \nover the IA-MO-IL triple point early this morning, with a 60 kt jet \nstreak still positioned over much of Missouri and extreme eastern \nKansas. This is helping to provide some lift for drizzle in northern \nforecast zones this morning, with overcast skies from lingering wrap \naround moisture. Dew point temperatures have already dropped into \nthe upper 50s and lower 60s across the region with, temperatures \ndipping into the lower 60s in some spots of the forecast area. With \nthe cyclone becoming vertically stacked, its eastward propagation \nhas slowed, which has been leaving much of the middle Mississippi \nRiver Valley and lower Missouri River Valley in a stronger pressure \ngradient. This, along with CAA and downward momentum transport, will \nallow gusty north to northwesterly winds through much of this \nafternoon, and should start to see this diminish as the low finally \npushes further eastward. Once the the last lobe of mid-level \nvorticity passes through, we should see any light sprinkle or \ndrizzle activity come to an end. The remainder of the weekend will \nbe marked by continued mid-level and upper-level northwesterly flow \nacross the area, with exiting system becoming vertically stacked \nover the southern Great Lakes Region and Ohio River Valley. While \nthis stalls there, an amplified H5 ridge develops over the western \nCONUS, with the Four Corners Region likely seeing a 594dam high \nsetup, with the ridge axis then being pushed northeastward into the \nUpper Midwest. This is reinforced by a short-wave disturbance \ndeveloping over western Canada. This setup over the weekend will \nresult in temperatures generally in the low to mid 80s across the \narea, with dewpoints in the lower to mid 60s.\n\nFor the next work week, the amplified mid-level ridge axis will \nforce most of the Pacific Northwest short-wave disturbances in the \nfar northern Plains and Canadian Prairie Provinces. Most deterministic \nsolutions have H5 height rises progged across much of the Central \nCONUS, with strong H85 WAA occurring. This will begin to push \ntemperatures back into the upper 80s and lower 90s, and ensemble \nprobabilities for highs reaching above 90F steadily rise as the week \nprogresses. Wednesday into Thursday, ridge axis will flatten as more \nshort-wave activity emanates from the Pacific Northwest, with CVA \ntracking more toward the Central Plains. Ensemble guidance is still \nspread on the potential for precipitation development, with some \nsolutions keeping the forcing much further north, while others are \nstarting to hint it may reach our northwestern zones. Probabilities \nfor measurable rainfall range from 15 to 30 percent, mainly for \nareas along Interstate 70 and northward. Next weekend, we are \nstaring to see an increasing signal for an unsettled weather pattern \nthat could bring multiple rounds of rain showers and thunderstorms. \nAt this time though, inner-quartile spread for both measurable \nrainfall and temperatures is quite large.",
